1. Classification by Data Processing Capabilities
This category defines how a computer processes internal signals and data types.
• Analog Computers: These process continuous, physical quantities that vary constantly (such as voltage, pressure, or temperature) instead of numerical digits.
	• Primary Examples: Analog speedometers, flight simulators, and mechanical thermometers.
• Digital Computers: These process information in discrete, binary formats using 0s and 1s. They execute mathematical operations and logical instructions at high speeds.
	• Primary Examples: Modern personal laptops, smartphones, and smart TVs.
• Hybrid Computers: These combine the features of both systems. They accept continuous analog inputs, convert them into binary, and process them with high digital precision.
	• Primary Examples: Heart rate monitors in hospital Intensive Care Units (ICUs) and electronic fuel pumps.
